  "explain": {
   "text": "Dividing by a fraction asks how many of that piece fit.[^1] How many 3/4 m tails in 6 m? Six metres is 24 quarters; each tail takes 3: 24 ÷ 3 = 8. So multiply by the fraction flipped, its [[reciprocal]]:[^2] 6 × 4/3 = 8.[^3]\n\nThe trap is expecting less. 6 × 3/4 gives 4 1/2 tails, using only 3.375 m. A piece under one fits more times than there are wholes.\n\n**Dividing by a fraction counts how many fit: 6 ÷ 3/4 = 6 × 4/3 = 8.**"
  },
  "analogy": {
   "text": "A 6 A circuit with lamps drawing a quarter of an amp each: 6 ÷ 1/4 = 24 lamps. Smaller draw, more lamps.",
   "breaks": "Lamps come whole: at 1 1/4 A each, 4.8 lamps means four, with 1 A spare."
  },
  "practice": {
   "text": "**Rule of thumb:** flip only the second number, then multiply.\n\n**Worked example:** a 15 m run from 2 1/2 m lengths. 2 1/2 is 5/2, flipped 2/5. 15 × 2/5 = 6 lengths. Check: 6 × 2.5 = 15.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Flipping the first number instead of the second.\n- Flipping 2 1/2 before writing it as 5/2."
  },
